# =============================================================================
# .trivyignore — Temporary Trivy suppressions
# =============================================================================
#
# Rules:
#   1. Every entry MUST have an expiration date (exp:YYYY-MM-DD).
#   2. Every entry MUST link to an upstream tracking issue/advisory.
#   3. When an entry expires, re-evaluate: remove it if the CVE is fixed,
#      or extend the date with a new justification if the fix still isn't
#      available. Never extend without re-checking upstream.
#   4. Keep the file short. A growing ignorefile is a smell.
#   5. Verify against the pinned CI Trivy (the `version` default in
#      .github/actions/install-trivy/action.yml) and its DB source —
#      local `brew install trivy` uses
#      the ghcr.io DB which can classify severities differently from the
#      CI's mirror.gcr.io/aquasec/trivy-db:2.
#
# Format:
#   CVE-XXXX-XXXXX exp:YYYY-MM-DD
# =============================================================================

# CVE-2026-17106 (GHSA-hfg8-hc9c-6c3h) — github.com/moby/go-archive v0.2.0: a
# crafted tar archive can write outside the extraction directory during
# untar. Statically linked into the docker-buildx CLI plugin that
# Dockerfile.dev installs (ARG BUILDX_VERSION). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the GCO dev image — the only scanned image
# that carries the Buildx plugin. Fixed in moby/go-archive 0.3.0.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the GHSA was published
# 2026-08-18T21:17Z and reached the Trivy DB in the following hours. The
# Security workflow was green on main at eeeab67 (2026-08-18T20:50Z), 27
# minutes before publication, so this appeared on an unchanged buildx binary.
#
# Not clearable by a stable pin bump: re-checked 2026-10-08 against the now
# current stable Buildx v0.38.0 (2026-10-07), which this repository pins.
# Upstream published go-archive v0.3.0 on 2026-07-30 (v0.3.3 by 2026-08-05),
# but Buildx has not adopted it — v0.38.0 still links v0.2.0, verified with
# `go version -m` on the published linux-amd64 binary (checksum matched the
# release's checksums.txt). The advisory range is `< 0.3.0`, so the v0.37.1
# -> v0.38.0 bump neither clears nor worsens the finding.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path is tar extraction. buildx is a
# build-time CLI plugin that extracts contexts and cache artifacts authored by
# the contributor or CI invoking it, plus the version-pinned images it pulls —
# not attacker-supplied archives. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that
# is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when a buildx release ships requiring moby/go-archive >= 0.3.0: bump
# BUILDX_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (the monthly deps-scan tracks it) and drop
# this entry. Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-17106
CVE-2026-17106 exp:2026-12-14

# CVE-2026-102276 (GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p) and CVE-2026-102278
# (GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7): brace-expansion denial of service by stack
# exhaustion, through recursion in parseCommaParts() and on nested brace
# groups. Fixed upstream in brace-expansion 5.0.10 and 5.0.11. npm ships its
# own brace-expansion copy inside its node_modules in the dev image, surfaced
# by security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev.
#
# CVE-2026-19534 (GHSA-rfgv-xxqx-mfg5): the undici WebSocket client throws an
# uncaught TypeError, ending the process, when a server's 101 response names
# a subprotocol the client never requested. Fixed upstream in undici 6.28.1.
# Present only as undici vendored inside npm's own node_modules for node-gyp,
# which uses undici's fetch to download Node headers and never opens a
# WebSocket.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: all three were published on
# 2026-09-29. The Security workflow was green on PR #429's head (3335db58) at
# 2026-09-30T01:56Z and red on its squash merge 50d86cd2, an identical tree,
# at 09:24Z. The advisories reached the Trivy DB in between.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-10-08 against npm 12.2.0 (the
# latest release, 2026-09-30, and the ARG NPM_VERSION this repository now
# pins) by reading the package.json files inside its published tarball — it
# vendors brace-expansion 5.0.9 and undici 6.28.0, so the bump from 12.0.2
# clears none of the three. The same bump did re-vendor ip-address 10.5.0 and
# tar 7.5.22 and lifted brace-expansion past 5.0.9, which retired the
# CVE-2026-14257, CVE-2026-69152, CVE-2026-69192 and CVE-2026-73566 entries
# this file used to carry. The root npm graph's separate brace-expansion
# copy, bundled by aws-cdk-lib, is suppressed in .npm-audit-ignore.
#
# Low runtime risk: the brace expansion runs on contributor/CI-authored globs
# in npm's own CLI handling, not untrusted remote input, and the undici path
# needs a WebSocket connection npm never makes. The dev image is a
# contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.11
# and undici >= 6.28.1: bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the
# packageManager pins the consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop these
# entries.
# Advisories: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-102276
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-102278
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-19534
CVE-2026-102276 exp:2026-11-21
CVE-2026-102278 exp:2026-11-21
CVE-2026-19534 exp:2026-11-21

# CVE-2026-109869 (GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c): undici does not handle an error on a
# response body stream, so a server that breaks the stream mid-body can crash
# the client process. Fixed upstream in undici 6.29.1, 7.30.1 and 8.12.2.
# Present only as the undici 6.28.0 vendored inside npm's own node_modules in
# the dev image (the same copy as CVE-2026-19534 above), surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: published 2026-10-23T22:14Z. The
# Security workflow was green on main at 42721a63 (2026-10-23T12:51Z) and
# first failed on PR #484, whose diff does not touch the dev image's npm.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-10-25 by reading
# node_modules/undici/package.json inside each published npm 12.x tarball.
# npm 12.2.0 (the pinned ARG NPM_VERSION) vendors undici 6.28.0 and npm 12.2.1
# (2026-10-15, the latest release) vendors 6.29.0, both below the fix.
#
# Low runtime risk: npm uses undici's fetch only for node-gyp downloading Node
# headers from the official dist server; the impact is availability of a
# contributor/CI command, and the dev image is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors undici >= 6.29.1: bump ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev together with the packageManager pins and drop
# this entry along with CVE-2026-19534.
# Advisory: https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c
CVE-2026-109869 exp:2026-11-25

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools 70.3.0, fixed 78.1.1) and GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# (msgpack 1.1.2, fixed 1.2.1) — reported by security:trivy:container-scan
# against the built dev image, under its python-pkg target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-09-08 and correcting the AWS CLI attribution
# this entry previously carried: both come from pip's vendored dependency
# manifest in the base image, at
# usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt, which pins
#   msgpack==1.1.2
#   setuptools==70.3.0
# Trivy's python-pkg analyzer parses that file, which is why neither package has
# a dist-info of its own and neither appears in `pip list` — they are vendored,
# not installed. Reproduced on the completely unmodified base image:
#   trivy image python:3.14.7-slim
# reports exactly these two ids and nothing else, with no repository content,
# no AWS CLI and no apt package involved.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — re-checked 2026-10-08 on the base this
# repository now pins: python:3.14.8-slim still ships pip 26.2.1, which is the
# newest pip on PyPI (2026-08-04), and its vendor.txt still pins both affected
# versions. `pip install --upgrade pip` is a no-op here, and no base-image
# bump can help because no fixed pip exists yet. pip's main branch has since
# re-vendored msgpack 1.2.1 (so the GHSA clears with the next pip release)
# but still pins setuptools 70.3.0. APT_SECURITY_EPOCH cannot reach this
# either: it only refreshes .deb packages, and this is a python-pkg finding.
#
# The earlier AWS CLI attribution was wrong, and bumping AWSCLI_VERSION
# 2.36.36 -> 2.36.41 on the strength of it did not clear either id. Recorded so
# the dead end is not re-walked: the 2.36.41 bundle and its installed
# /usr/local/aws-cli layout contain one dist-info (prompt_toolkit), zero
# egg-info, zero PKG-INFO, no msgpack file and no file containing "70.3.0", and
# a pinned-Trivy scan of that bundle is clean. Our own tree is clear too —
# msgpack appears nowhere in requirements-lock.txt and setuptools only as a
# commented "considered unsafe" marker.
#
# Low runtime risk, and for setuptools the vulnerable code is not even shipped:
#   * CVE-2025-47273 is a path traversal in setuptools.package_index.PackageIndex.
#     pip vendors pkg_resources, not setuptools — there is no
#     pip/_vendor/setuptools directory in the image, so the flagged module is
#     absent and vendor.txt records the version for provenance only.
#   * msgpack 1.1.2 code is present at pip/_vendor/msgpack, reached only through
#     cachecontrol serialising pip's own HTTP cache while pip talks to a package
#     index. Impact is a crash of pip (availability only, no code execution).
#   * The dev image is contributor/CI tooling that is never deployed to a
#     cluster, and neither path runs as a network service.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.1 and setuptools >= 78.1.1 (or drops
# the setuptools line): pull a base image carrying that pip, confirm with
# `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Because the finding is
# entirely upstream of this repository, a published VEX statement would be the
# durable alternative to re-dating this entry.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-47273
#             https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-12-14
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-97687 (traffic interception via an HTTPS proxy TLS configuration
# override) and CVE-2026-97689 (denial of service through unbounded memory
# allocation in the chunked-transfer parser) — urllib3 2.7.0, fixed in 2.8.0.
# Reported by security:trivy:container-scan against the dev image
# (Dockerfile.dev) and the two Lambda images built on
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (helm-installer, tls-certificate-manager),
# under the pathless "Python (python-pkg)" target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-10-01: the same mechanism as the entry above.
# pip 26.2.1 vendors urllib3 and records it in
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt as `urllib3==2.7.0`; Trivy parses that manifest, so
# the finding has no dist-info of its own. Reproduced on the unmodified base
# images: `pip --version` reports 26.2.1 in both python:3.14.7-slim
# (/usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages) and public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14
# (/var/lang/lib/python3.14/site-packages), and both vendor.txt files pin
# urllib3==2.7.0. The images that keep no pip in their final stage do not
# report it. Our own urllib3 is 2.8.0 everywhere Trivy can see it:
# requirements-lock.txt, every lambda/*/requirements.txt, and each image's
# urllib3-2.8.0.dist-info scans clean in the same reports.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — re-checked 2026-10-08: pip 26.2.1
# (2026-08-04) is still the newest pip on PyPI, python:3.14.8-slim (the base
# this repository now pins) ships that same pip with `urllib3==2.7.0` in its
# vendor.txt, and pip's main branch still vendors urllib3 2.7.0, so no fixed
# pip exists to upgrade to. A base-image bump cannot help until pip
# re-vendors, and APT_SECURITY_EPOCH only refreshes .deb packages.
#
# Low runtime risk: pip's vendored urllib3 runs only while pip itself talks to
# a package index, which the Lambda images never do after build and which the
# dev image does only while a contributor installs packages. Neither CVE is
# reachable through the application code, which imports the fixed 2.8.0.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors urllib3 >= 2.8.0: pull a base image carrying that
# pip, confirm with `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Expires
# together with the sibling pip vendor.txt entry above so the two are
# re-evaluated in one pass.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97687
#             https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97689
CVE-2026-97687 exp:2026-12-14
CVE-2026-97689 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three advisories from the 2026-10-08 Go security release, fixed in Go 1.26.9
# and 1.27.2:
#   * CVE-2026-78667 (GHSA-f4xw-m386-m277, GO-2026-6609): net/http puts no
#     limit on the ranges it parses from a Range header, so FileServer,
#     ServeContent and ServeFile can be driven to excessive CPU use.
#   * CVE-2026-97031 (GHSA-hq7w-2xrq-wp2j, GO-2026-6607): a crypto/tls server
#     with Encrypted Client Hello enabled accepts repeated ECH outer extension
#     references, which a client can use to exhaust the server's memory.
#   * CVE-2026-78669 (GO-2026-6611): an HTTP/2 peer that opens many streams
#     and then sends many small SETTINGS frames carrying
#     SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE can drive a client or a server to excessive
#     CPU use. It is also in golang.org/x/net/http2, fixed in x/net 0.60.0
#     (2026-10-08).
# Trivy reports them as `stdlib` (and CVE-2026-78669 also as golang.org/x/net)
# under each Go binary's gobinary target in security:trivy:container-scan:
#   * Dockerfile.dev: kubectl v1.37.1 (go1.26.8, x/net v0.57.0) and the
#     docker-buildx plugin v0.38.0 (go1.26.8, x/net v0.59.0)
#   * lambda/helm-installer: kubectl v1.37.1 (as above) and the base image's
#     aws-lambda-rie (go1.26.7, no x/net)
#   * lambda/tls-certificate-manager: the base image's aws-lambda-rie
# The Docker CLI and helm no longer carry them: on 2026-10-19 DOCKER_VERSION
# moved from 29.8.2 to 29.9.1 (go1.26.9, no x/net) and helm from v4.3.0 to
# v4.3.1 (go1.27.2, x/net v0.60.0).
#
# Provenance, re-established 2026-10-19: `go version -m` on the exact pinned
# linux-amd64 release artifacts, each checked against the sha256 trust anchor
# its Dockerfile carries (the Docker static tarball publishes none), reports
# the toolchains and x/net versions above, and a local Trivy scan of the same
# artifacts reports all three ids for kubectl v1.37.1 and helm v4.3.0 and none
# for Docker 29.9.1 or helm v4.3.1. aws-lambda-rie is not ours: it ships in
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 at /usr/local/bin/aws-lambda-rie, and the
# copy in the current base (created 2026-10-15) still reports go1.26.7.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisories were published
# 2026-10-08T22:31Z, and the GHSAs for the first two at 2026-10-09T00:31Z.
# They reached the Trivy DB at different times: PR #451's Security runs
# scanned the same three images clean at 2026-10-09T01:55Z (7b7b4b78), and
# clean of CVE-2026-78669 as late as 14:14Z (b6686e5c).
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-10-19: Kubernetes v1.37.1
# (2026-09-23) is still the newest 1.37 patch (the October patch release,
# targeted for 2026-10-13, has not shipped, and the next target is
# 2026-11-10), Buildx v0.38.0 (2026-10-07) is still the newest Buildx
# release, and the newest upstream RIE, v1.37 (2026-08-31), predates both
# fixed Go releases. The 2026-11-08 expiry was extended for that reason: it
# fell before any of the three could be fixed.
#
# Low runtime risk. CVE-2026-78667 needs the process to serve files through
# net/http to a peer that controls the Range header, and CVE-2026-97031 needs
# a TLS server with ECH keys configured: GCO runs kubectl and buildx only as
# clients (of the Kubernetes API, image registries and the Docker daemon) and
# never starts kubectl's file-serving `proxy --www` mode. CVE-2026-78669 does
# reach clients, but only from the HTTP/2 peer itself, which for these
# clients is a TLS endpoint they chose to call (the Kubernetes API or an
# image registry). The worst case is CPU exhaustion of one CLI process, or of
# one helm-installer invocation, which the Lambda timeout ends.
# aws-lambda-rie runs only when AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API is unset, which means
# local emulation such as the integration:docker smoke tests
# (.github/scripts/rie_smoke_test.sh), where it answers plain-HTTP invokes on
# an ephemeral CI runner. Deployed Lambda functions exec the managed runtime
# and never start it. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is never
# deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when kubectl, buildx and the base image's RIE are built with Go >=
# 1.26.9 (or >= 1.27.2) and, where they link x/net, with golang.org/x/net >=
# 0.60.0: the next Kubernetes 1.37 patch (KUBECTL_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev,
# plus the kubectl URL and sha256 in lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile), the
# next Buildx release (BUILDX_VERSION and both sha256 anchors), and a
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 whose aws-lambda-rie is built with a
# fixed Go. Confirm each with `go version -m` on the binary (or
# `trivy image`), then drop all three ids.
# Advisories: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6609
#             https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6607
#             https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6611
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-78667
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-97031
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-78669
# RIE releases: https://github.com/aws/aws-lambda-runtime-interface-emulator/releases
CVE-2026-78667 exp:2026-12-14
CVE-2026-97031 exp:2026-12-14
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-56851 (GO-2026-6629): the Nickname profile in
# golang.org/x/text/secure/precis can panic with an out-of-bounds slice error
# when it transforms crafted input into a short destination buffer. Fixed in
# golang.org/x/text 0.41.0. security:trivy:container-scan reports it for
# kubectl v1.37.1, which links x/text v0.40.0, in Dockerfile.dev and
# lambda/helm-installer. No other scanned binary links an affected x/text.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisory was published
# 2026-10-07T14:09Z, but the Security run on main at 972fa63e (started
# 2026-10-16T18:34Z) still scanned both images clean. The first run to
# report it was PR #466's at 2026-10-19T10:17Z, with the same kubectl pin.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-10-19: v1.37.1 is still the
# newest Kubernetes 1.37 patch (next target 2026-11-10; see the entry above).
#
# Not reachable: the vulnerable package is not compiled into kubectl. The
# release binary is stripped, but Go keeps every linked function's name for
# stack traces. Those names cover golang.org/x/text's cases, encoding,
# internal/language, language, runes, secure/bidirule, transform,
# unicode/bidi and unicode/norm packages, and nothing from
# golang.org/x/text/secure/precis, whose Profile and Transformer methods the
# advisory lists as the vulnerable symbols. govulncheck cannot confirm this
# on a stripped binary: it falls back to module-level matching and reports
# every symbol. Even where linked, the impact is a panic of one CLI process.
#
# Clears when a kubectl release links golang.org/x/text >= 0.41.0 (check with
# `go version -m`): bump it as the entry above describes, then drop this id.
# Expires with the entry above so the kubectl pins are re-evaluated in one
# pass.
# Advisory: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6629
CVE-2026-56851 exp:2026-12-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2: certifi before 2026.10.18 bundles emSign ECC Root CA -
# G3, and eight eMudhra intermediates that chain to it have compromised keys
# (the CA has revoked them). certifi 2026.10.19 drops that root. Reported by
# security:trivy:container-scan against the dev image and both Lambda images
# built on public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (helm-installer,
# tls-certificate-manager), as certifi 2026.6.17 under the pathless
# "Python (python-pkg)" target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-10-21: the same mechanism as the two pip
# vendor.txt entries above. pip 26.2.1 vendors certifi and records it in
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt as `certifi==2026.6.17`. Reproduced on the
# unmodified bases: python:3.14.8-slim (rebuilt 2026-10-21) and
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (rebuilt 2026-10-20) both ship pip
# 26.2.1, which is also the pip their ensurepip bundles. Our own certifi is
# 2026.10.19 (requirements-lock.txt since #470), and the dev image's
# certifi-2026.10.19.dist-info scans clean in the same report; the Lambda
# images install no certifi of their own.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the advisory was published
# 2026-10-20T19:35Z, and the Security run on main at 879bec75 (started
# 2026-10-20T21:56Z) still scanned all three images clean.
#
# Not clearable by a pin we hold: pip 26.2.2 (2026-10-20) re-vendors certifi
# 2026.10.19 and changes nothing else in vendor.txt, but neither base ships it
# yet, and the images take pip from their base rather than pinning one.
#
# Low runtime risk: pip trusts the vendored bundle alongside the system store
# while it talks to a package index, which the Lambda images do only at build
# time and the dev image only while a contributor installs packages.
# Exploiting it needs an on-path position on that connection plus one of the
# revoked intermediate keys.
#
# Clears when both bases ship pip >= 26.2.2: confirm with `trivy image` on
# each base alone, then drop the id. Upgrading pip inside the three images
# would clear it sooner, at the cost of a pin no scan tracks.
# Advisory: https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2
GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2 exp:2026-11-21

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-111943 (GO-2026-6809): Go 1.26.10 and 1.27.3 add a denylist to
# crypto/x509, so Certificate.Verify rejects a chain through any of eight
# revoked eMudhra intermediates (the keys behind the certifi entry above).
# Trivy reports it as `stdlib` under each Go binary's gobinary target in
# security:trivy:container-scan:
#   * Dockerfile.dev: the Docker CLI 29.9.1 (go1.26.9), kubectl v1.37.1
#     (go1.26.8) and the docker-buildx plugin v0.38.0 (go1.26.8)
#   * lambda/helm-installer: helm v4.3.1 (go1.27.2), kubectl v1.37.1 and the
#     base image's aws-lambda-rie (go1.26.9)
#   * lambda/tls-certificate-manager: the base image's aws-lambda-rie
# The kubectl and helm release artifacts, matched to the sha256 anchors in
# lambda/helm-installer/Dockerfile, carry the toolchains above, and so does
# the aws-lambda-rie in public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 as rebuilt on
# 2026-10-20. That rebuild moved the RIE from go1.26.7 to go1.26.9, which the
# 2026-10-08 entry above names as fixed; kubectl and buildx still hold that
# entry open.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the Go advisory was published
# 2026-10-21T00:27Z, and the Security run on main at 879bec75 (started
# 2026-10-20T21:56Z) still scanned all three images clean.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump, re-checked 2026-10-21: kubectl v1.37.1 is still
# the newest Kubernetes 1.37 patch, helm v4.3.1 (2026-10-15) is the newest
# Helm 4 release, and the newest upstream RIE, v1.37 (2026-08-31), predates
# the fix. Docker 29.9.2 and Buildx v0.38.1 (both 2026-10-20) are built with
# go1.26.10, but this id is suppressed in every image at once, so moving those
# two pins alone clears nothing; take them with the bump that clears the rest.
#
# Low runtime risk: the denylist matters only when a binary verifies a server
# chain against a trust store that still holds the root those intermediates
# chain to, and an on-path attacker holds one of the revoked keys.
#   * kubectl, and helm's Kubernetes calls, verify the EKS API against the
#     cluster CA in the kubeconfig's certificate-authority-data, not against
#     the system store.
#   * helm's chart downloads in the helm-installer Lambda do use the system
#     store, and Amazon Linux 2023's ca-certificates
#     (2025.2.76-1.0.amzn2023.0.3, the newest in its repository on
#     2026-10-21) still carries emSign ECC Root CA - G3. That is the one
#     exposed path, from private subnets through the VPC's NAT gateways.
#   * python:3.14.8-slim as rebuilt on 2026-10-21 ships Debian's
#     ca-certificates 20250419+deb13u1, which no longer carries that root,
#     so the dev image's Docker CLI, buildx and kubectl cannot build such a
#     chain. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that is never deployed
#     to a cluster.
#   * aws-lambda-rie runs only when AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API is unset, which
#     means local emulation; deployed functions never start it.
#
# Clears when kubectl, helm and the Lambda base's aws-lambda-rie are built
# with Go >= 1.26.10 (or >= 1.27.3) and DOCKER_VERSION and BUILDX_VERSION in
# Dockerfile.dev move to releases built with a fixed Go (29.9.2 and v0.38.1
# already are). Confirm each binary's toolchain (or run `trivy image`), then
# drop the id.
# Advisories: https://pkg.go.dev/vuln/GO-2026-6809
#             https://go.dev/issue/82345
CVE-2026-111943 exp:2026-11-21
